    kind: work_item  # 64ad
    title: "database_query linker: a SQL call site naming two or more tables gets a name and node id that depend on PYTHONHASHSEED (tables joined from a set, unsorted)"  # 64ad
    status: todo_hard  # 64ad
    priority: 3  # 64ad
    parent:  # 64ad
    tags:  # 64ad
    - determinism  # 64ad
    - reproducibility  # 64ad
    - linkers  # 64ad
    isbefore: []  # 64ad
    duplicate_of: []  # 64ad
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 64ad
    pr_ref:  # 64ad
    description: "FOUND 2026-11-15 while comparing two analyses of one tree (dev e2b6ab8096 source, same analyzer apart from an unrelated two-line change in py.py).\n\nWHAT. linkers/database_query.py collects the tables of a SQL statement in a set (line 170) and builds the call-site symbol's name by joining them unsorted (line 362: tables_str = \", \".join(pattern.tables)). The name is part of the node id. So the name and the id of a call site whose statement names two or more tables depend on set iteration order, which changes from process to process.\n\nOBSERVED. packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/selection_index.py:793 and :1165 (SELECT ... FROM file f JOIN block b ...): one run names the call sites \"SELECT file, block\", the other \"SELECT block, file\". Two node ids and four uses edges differ between the two surveys for this reason alone (~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/naviz_agda_investigation_11152026/naviz/self_cmp.txt). The stable_id is not affected: line 388 sorts the tables.\n\nFIX DIRECTION. Sort the tables where the name is built (as line 388 does), or keep them in statement order by collecting into a list. Then sweep the other linkers for a name or id built by joining a set.\n\nDONE WHEN. Two runs of one tree under different PYTHONHASHSEED values give the same name and id for a multi-table statement (a test that runs the linker in two subprocesses with fixed, different seeds), and the sweep's result is recorded.\n\nRELATED. Same family: WI-fopug-* (validator message iterates a str set), WI-rumuv-* (napi export map filled in set order); both open."  # 64ad
    fields: {}  # 64ad
